WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.645 --> 00:00:12.020
MICHAEL ANTON:
Welcome, everyone,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.860
to our second panel of the day,
of the morning on the future

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.270
of the Republican Party.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.590
My name is Michael Anton.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.950
I am lecturer and research
fellow at the Kirby Center,

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.420
of Hillsdale College's Kirby
Center here in Washington, DC,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.120
just a few miles from here,
where, as you heard last night,

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.060
we are launching a brand
new program this week,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.920
a graduate school for
statesmanship and politics.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.770
And it's actually
already underway.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.680
We've got some students
there studying,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.030
and we expect this to grow
into something really,

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.700
really big and important.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.930
And I'm excited to
be a part of it.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.760
I'm really excited
to be a part of it.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.910
It's one of the reasons
that I wanted to come here,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.780
and was very glad when I
was hired to come here.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.720
So I know all three of
the people in this panel.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.569
So it'll be very easy for me to
introduce them without notes.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:08.977
David Azerrad is a fellow at
the Heritage Foundation, a dear

00:01:08.977 --> 00:01:10.560
friend of mine, for
what that's worth.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.230
He may want to disavow it.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.480
And he's been spending a lot of
his time lately thinking deeply

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.210
about partisan politics, and
particularly the intersection

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.120
of identity politics
and what effect

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.100
that has on the American
two-party system

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.190
and on the political
system more broadly.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.340
Allen Guelzo is a longtime
historian and professor

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.130
at Gettysburg College who
has also recently taken up

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.410
an affiliation with
Princeton University.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.020
So that institution has
risen in my estimation

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.630
by the nature of that hire.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.750
I can think of few
people, especially

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.520
as an old student
of Lincoln myself,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.400
I think of few people
better equipped

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.370
to talk about the future
of the Republican Party

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.160
simply because I think there's
hardly a person on Earth,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.560
if there is anyone, who
knows more about the origins

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.600
and the history of
the Republican Party

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.650
than Professor Guelzo.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.320
And therefore, he's
very well positioned

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.520
to speak about its future.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.040
He's also, if you've never heard
him speak, every speech he does

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.278
is a bit of performance
art in the best sense.

00:02:09.278 --> 00:02:11.070
I don't know how he
does it because there's

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.620
no tapes of these
19th century orators.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.970
But I imagine when I hear him--

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.512
I remember the first
time I heard him

00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:18.720
I was quite blown
away by how polished,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.000
practiced, and really
impactful it was--

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.600
that somehow he unearthed
tapes before recording devices

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.790
were even invented,
and he learned

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.940
how the speakers of the
past traded in their art.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.030
And I was quite amazed, and
I think you probably will be,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.630
too.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:38.275
Henry Olsen, another dear
friend of mine for many years,

00:02:38.275 --> 00:02:39.900
I think he's here
for a lot of reasons,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.270
but one overarching
reason is there's just

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.120
nobody who better understands
the political tides

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.630
and currents at a very
granular, district by district,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.520
percentage points numbers level.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.230
And he can take hard, confusing,
maybe even intimidating

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.140
and boring data and
make it extremely

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.828
accessible and
understandable, in terms

00:03:02.828 --> 00:03:04.620
that an audience that
maybe doesn't want to

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.470
swim around in spreadsheets
all day looking at numbers.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.970
Those eyes might
glaze over at that.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.262
I would include myself in that.

00:03:10.262 --> 00:03:13.560
He can take that and put it
into words in a way that just

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.970
makes sense of all of that.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.540
And just simply
the best election

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.115
watcher I know right now.

00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:22.740
I would say also, he's a fellow
at the Ethics and Public Policy

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.490
Center, and he writes a daily
column in the Washington Post.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.880
Again, another reason to
think slightly more highly

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.790
of that institution, which
is, if you want to know,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.070
if you want to know really
what's going on in the run

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:40.590
up to the 2020 election in
detail, follow that column.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.942
So I am the chair, and I think
I have fulfilled my duties.

00:03:44.942 --> 00:03:47.400
My only other duty is to make
sure everybody stays on time.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.858
That means I'm going to give
them the hook if they go over.

00:03:49.858 --> 00:03:51.300
We are going in
the order you see.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.281
That means Mr. Azerrad
is your first speaker.

00:03:55.281 --> 00:03:58.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:00.325 --> 00:04:02.200
DAVID AZERRAD: Thank
you, Mike, and thank you

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.950
to Hillsdale for inviting me.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.710
It's quite an honor
to be participating

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.960
in this Constitution
Day celebration.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.180
I was not so lucky as
to go to Hillsdale.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.920
They don't have a campus in
Canada last time I checked.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.700
But I did go to grad school
at the University of Dallas,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.200
where I had the
pleasure of studying

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.260
under Tom West
and RJ [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.940
and with Kevin Slack.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.130
I then worked under Matt
Spalding at the Heritage

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.690
Foundation for three years.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.480
And over the years
at Heritage, I've

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.360
had the pleasure of teaching
many Hillsdale interns,

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:38.810
and I'm always impressed by how
well their minds and character

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.340
are well-formed.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.670
Most universities,
it seems to me,

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.320
are in the business of
delivering credentials.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.360
Hillsdale is in the
business of ensuring

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.700
that they get an education.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.530
Regrettably, I was
not asked to talk

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.210
about the life of
the mind, but to deal

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.900
in much less dignified
subject, albeit one

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.400
that is quite important, namely
the future of the Republican

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.070
Party.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:04.220
As I see it, there is
a fundamental asymmetry

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.220
between our two political
parties in America.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.000
I'll state the matter bluntly so
as to highlight the difference.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.240
The Republican Party wants
to win elections in order

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.260
to enact Republican policies,
whereas the Democratic Party

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:22.280
wants to win elections to ensure
that the Republican Party never

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.930
wins another election.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.530
The Democrats, and the
left more generally,

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:31.340
have flipped von Clausewitz's
maxim on its head.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.650
Politics for them
is the continuation

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.540
of war by other means.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.970
The goal is to defeat the enemy.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.010
Now, in the immediate
sense, the enemy

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:43.010
is defined as the
Republican Party, especially

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.770
the racist orange man who
was installed by the Russians

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.420
in the White House.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.150
But Trump and the GOP
find their support

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.760
amongst all of the deplorables
who cling to their guns

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.060
and their religion,
conservative media, and all

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.520
of the institutions
of the vast right wing

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.370
conspiracy that occupies
the progressive imagination.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.160
They, too, must be
brought to heel.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.820
These enemies need
not be annihilated,

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.160
but they must be subdued.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.140
The GOP and the
right may continue

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:16.370
to exist, but only as a
controlled, neutered opposition

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.370
that does not challenge
in any meaningful way

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.640
the progressive project
to fundamentally transform

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.930
the United States of America
into a romanticized version

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:28.130
of Sweden.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:31.220
I say romanticized because
Sweden has universal school

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:34.130
choice and private health
care, no elective late term

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.200
abortions or minimum
wage, and it does not

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.420
bestow birthright
citizenship upon the children

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:39.860
of illegal aliens.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.320
This, however, never
figures in Bernie Sanders'

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.350
glowing accounts of the nation.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.960
As a result of this
asymmetry, there

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.850
is a clarity of purpose,
a resolve, and dare I say,

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:56.000
a ruthlessness in
the Democratic Party

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.510
that one rarely finds
in the Republican Party.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:01.370
They are at war with us.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.380
They have openly said so.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:06.740
But too many of us
prefer not to admit this.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.620
They play to win
while we just play

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.140
maybe not to lose too badly.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.950
I was at a dinner with
several prominent conservative

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:18.650
intellectuals in
Washington, DC, last month.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.270
And the consensus
around the table

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.870
was we should use whatever
political power we now

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:27.230
have to enact policies,
like school choice, that

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:31.040
will shield us from what will
come once Elizabeth Warren

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.650
or Kamala Harris
become president.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.110
Now, listen, this is not
a foolish way to think,

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:37.910
this I readily concede.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:40.130
But here is the question
that I put to the table

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.530
and that I would put
to you this morning.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.970
Do you think anyone on the
left ever thinks like this?

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.380
Do you think they ever
ask themselves, well,

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.370
what can we do to make sure
that they're not mean to us when

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.930
they win an election?

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.530
Of course they don't.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:57.580
Because Democrats don't
think like losers.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.880
Because Democrats are not
afraid of Republicans.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.590
And can you blame them?

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:06.500
At most, the GOP will
thwart for a few years

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.910
their great ambitions, like
Medicare for all, amnesty

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:11.980
for all, or the Green New Deal.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:14.440
But it's not like they have
to fear that the Republican

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:19.420
Party is ever going to repeal
any one of their major programs

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.130
once it's implemented.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:25.600
Need I remind you that the
GOP promised for eight years

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.050
to repeal and replace
Obamacare, and when they finally

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.740
had the opportunity to
do so, they flinched.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.980
The first order of business,
therefore, for the GOP--

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.380
if it wants to have a future--

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.809
is an attitude adjustment.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:44.950
We need a more spirited
Republican Party, a Republican

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.470
Party that fights with
the same intensity

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:51.250
and clarity of purpose as
the Democratic Party does,

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:54.610
a Republican Party
that fights to win.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:58.000
And this is one area where the
current figurehead of the party

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.790
has much to teach his
fellow Republicans.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:04.510
Now in praising him, I am not
blind to his shortcomings.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.727
President Trump's flaws
are readily apparent,

00:09:07.727 --> 00:09:10.060
and let me remind you, they
have received more attention

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.310
than those of any other
president in American history.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.030
He does, however, also
possess certain virtues

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.970
that are worthy of
emulation, chief of which

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.710
is his spiritedness.

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:24.010
The president may not always
pick his battles well,

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.800
nor does he always fight
them in the most prudent way,

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.800
or with decorum for
that matter, but no one

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.200
can question his will to fight.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:36.100
This I suspect accounts
for much of his popularity

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:39.310
amongst the Republican base
and certain independents,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.110
and it accounts for
much of the horror

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.270
he inspires in Democrats.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.200
A Republican who fights
unapologetically?

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.790
What a sight!

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.010
This is not what we
have come to expect

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:54.970
from the party that gave us
Mitt Romney, Paul Ryan, and Jeb

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:56.290
Bush.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.300
Now, I realize that
some of you may

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.020
find my counsel distasteful.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.780
Wouldn't it be better,
you may ask, if, you know,

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.170
Trump and the GOP
didn't escalate things?

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.620
Wouldn't it be nicer if we had
a more mild-mannered person

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:11.710
at the head of the party?

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:14.130
I don't know, maybe a gentleman?

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.190
We are living in
turbulent times.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.580
The country is in the
midst of a cold civil war,

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.950
and we are not dealing
with an opponent who

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.800
abides by the gentlemen's
code of conduct.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.460
The times call for more
aggressive men and women.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.530
Gentlemen are admirable
in many regards,

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.830
but they have their limitations.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.960
A gentleman, for
example, will not

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.530
be comfortable humiliating
a woman in public.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.720
This, you will admit,
poses a problem

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.200
if the woman in question
is Kamala Harris, Elizabeth

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.760
Warren, or who knows,
Michelle Obama.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:53.450
Now ultimately, it's not
enough to want to fight.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:55.420
You've got to fight
for the right things.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.503
And it's not enough to
fight for the right things.

00:10:57.503 --> 00:10:59.710
You've got to fight
through to victory.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:03.450
So what should the
Republican Party fight for?

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.240
The immediate priority,
it seems to me,

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.900
is to prevent the Democratic
Party and their allies

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.780
on the left from
enacting policies

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:18.960
that will all but guarantee
democratic political hegemony.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.040
They currently have their
sights set on three areas

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.280
to ensure that, and
these are further

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:29.250
restricting political
speech, transforming

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.560
the electoral
system, and changing

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.720
the demographics of the
country through continued

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:34.980
large-scale immigration.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.020
Allow me to explain them.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.510
First, on free speech,
America is currently

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:43.620
the only Western nation in the
world that has not criminalized

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.570
so-called "hate speech."

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:47.910
Now I say so-called
because at issue

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:51.630
is not racial slurs
or Holocaust denial,

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:53.880
but if we criminalize
hate speech,

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.640
we would essentially curtail
and to a large extent

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.550
forbid deliberation on
major political issues

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.230
confronting the country.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:04.650
In the left's expansive
conception of hate speech,

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:08.910
calling for less immigration,
advocating for welfare reform,

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.880
opposing the transgender
agenda, or simply defending

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.130
the Christian
conception of marriage

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:16.890
could all be considered
forms of hate speech.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.720
Let me put it to you bluntly.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.490
Hate speech,
according to the left,

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:23.430
encompasses an awful lot of
Republican, conservative,

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.540
and Christian speech.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.660
The Supreme Court, it
is true, has so far

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.600
stood strong on the
issue, but conservatives

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.350
must remain vigilant.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.370
The court, after all, is not
bound by its own precedence,

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.380
and it will often
bow to public opinion

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.720
and change its mind on a whim.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.550
Now, the left's
assault on free speech

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:45.810
goes well beyond restricting
what we are allowed to say.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:50.940
The Democratic Party also wants
to further restrict our ability

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.930
to fund political speech by
enacting ever tighter campaign

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.430
finance restrictions.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.580
The rhetoric they deploy
is quite seductive--

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.970
we want to have everyone
have an equal voice;

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:06.120
we want to end undue influence.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.580
But the effectual
truth of such policies,

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.710
as Machiavelli
might say, would be

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:14.580
to give the media an
even more outsized voice

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.350
in influencing elections.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.750
Because the press,
remember, is never

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.240
subject to campaign
finance laws.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.610
And while they like
to speak of "earned

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:27.840
media" for the various
candidates, don't be fooled.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:31.770
All media is bestowed
by the media.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:35.100
The First Amendment, as
understood by the framers,

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.840
protects not just
your right to speak,

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:40.950
but your right to publish
and promote your views.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.740
And a Republican Party
that wants to win

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.440
should therefore set its
sights on dismantling

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.587
most of the campaign
finance architecture

00:13:48.587 --> 00:13:50.670
that has been built up in
America in the past four

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:52.000
decades.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.650
We cannot win if we play by the
rules they rigged in their own

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.120
favor.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.520
Lastly, the
Republicans really need

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.920
to take seriously, look into
the very credible accusations

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.450
that the major tech platforms--

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.360
Twitter, Facebook, and
in particular, Google--

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:13.620
are influencing our politics by
manipulating what information

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.990
Americans can see online.

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:18.270
Right now, Senators
Cruz and Hawley

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.550
are basically the
only two vocal voices

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:23.160
in the party who take
this threat seriously.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.720
Big tech has a political agenda.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.830
These are not just regular
for-profit corporations.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.480
And that agenda is
not just progressive,

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.480
it is anti-conservative.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:36.480
But their activities
and their algorithms

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:38.760
are shrouded in secrecy.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:42.360
The Republican Party has
to wake up to the fact

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.660
that threats to liberty can
come from the private sector

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.190
and not just the state.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.160
Second, looking at
the electoral system,

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.370
the Democratic Party wants to
transform our electoral system

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:55.790
to skew it in its favor.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.700
And they want to do this
primarily by one of two ways.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.060
The first is to make
it easier for anyone

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.220
to vote by essentially
eliminating

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.660
any safeguards on voter fraud.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:07.270
Now concerns for
voter fraud, they

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.480
say, you know what they are?

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:12.460
They're racist dog whistles
to disenfranchise minorities.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:14.830
Now I gotta tell you,
I'm not a citizen yet.

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:15.640
I'm working on it.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.190
But as a foreigner
living in America,

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:22.030
that is the one thing I
absolutely cannot wrap my mind

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:22.930
about--

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:24.280
wrap my mound around--

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.025
wrap my mound around--

00:15:26.025 --> 00:15:28.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:33.660
--wrap my mind around about
this great country I so admire.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.450
How in God's green Earth
can you allow people

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.490
to vote without showing ID?

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:41.400
How can you not demand proof of
citizenship when it comes time

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:42.090
to register?

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.320
And while I'm at
it, how do you not

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:48.030
check whether people
are registered

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:49.620
in more than one state?

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:51.120
It's surreal.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.490
Even we silly Canadians
don't do this.

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:54.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:58.560
The Democratic Party basically
wants an honor system

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.990
when it comes to voting,
the result of it which

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.330
would be maximum fraud.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:07.080
It therefore has to be a
top priority for the GOP,

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.570
especially at the
state level, to protect

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.700
the integrity of the ballot.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.920
But the Democrats don't
just want everyone to vote,

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.250
including, by the way,
incarcerated felons.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:21.120
You can thank Bernie Sanders and
the ACLU for that genius idea.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.490
They also want to get rid
of that pesky Electoral

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:26.310
College that keeps on
allowing the GOP to steal

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:29.760
elections the way they
did in 2000 and 2016.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.790
Therefore, they are actively
pushing the National Popular

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:34.080
Vote initiative.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.810
It's now been adopted by
15 states and the District

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:40.740
of Columbia, and the left is
pouring millions of dollars

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.470
into this initiative
that must be countered.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.980
Meanwhile, the Republican Party
has devoted more resources

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:48.420
to defending the
carried interest

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:52.160
loophole than to defending
the Electoral College.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.880
Then there is this question
of demographic change.

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.580
So we conservatives love
to quote Andrew Breitbart.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.230
You know the dictum, right?

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.330
Culture is upstream
from politics,

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.310
and this is our way
of excusing ourselves

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:06.770
for having lost so
much ground in America

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.390
over the past century.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:10.190
But it seems to me
that this is not

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:13.380
how the left and the Democratic
Party look at things.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:17.300
They seem to think that
demographics is upstream

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:21.440
from both culture and politics,
and that if they can change

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:23.869
the demographics of
the country, primarily

00:17:23.869 --> 00:17:26.060
through large-scale
immigration, they can

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.580
secure a permanent majority.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:31.730
Now, it's no secret that
immigrants and their children

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:34.880
overwhelmingly
vote for Democrats.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.450
So when the Democrats say
diversity is our strength,

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:39.990
you better believe them.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.140
It's just that the
our in the sentence

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:45.200
is not America, but
the Democratic Party.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.670
And so they want to
abolish the border.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.360
They want to attract even more
immigrants with the promise

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:52.190
of free health care
and a reassurance

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.860
that in America we
don't enforce our laws.

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:58.400
All of this paving the way
for a glorious amnesty that

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:02.360
would turn Texas, Florida,
and Arizona into California.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:04.250
Now I'm not saying
that Republicans

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:07.680
should oppose all
immigration because of this.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.460
I just think that, when
the enemy is giving you

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:15.230
his war plans, you'd be foolish
not to take them seriously.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:17.810
I don't know what the future
demographics of the country

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:20.990
are, but I'm not a
Republican strategist.

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:23.360
What worries me is that
the Republican strategists

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:25.550
don't seem to think
about this either.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.590
At the very least,
it seems to me

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:30.260
that Republicans should
hold the line strong

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:32.880
and oppose any and all amnesty.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.270
The last time we
did amnesty in '86,

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:37.640
there were 5 million
illegals in this country.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:42.770
Today, there are anywhere
between 11 and 29 million.

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:44.600
Now, I'll end by saying this--

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:47.900
these three priorities
are the bare minimum

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.150
if the GOP just wants
to hold the line,

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:52.320
if it wants to have a future.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:55.640
But these are not going
to constitute a victory.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.280
The GOP also needs to
go on the offensive,

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.100
and that really means
thinking like the left

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.940
and going after the centers
of power of the enemy.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:08.090
And in this case,
it's the universities.

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:10.520
Hillsdale and a handful
of other colleges

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:16.040
excepted, our universities
are deeply, deeply corrupt.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:18.230
They do not serve
the common good.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.750
They do not promote
the discovery of truth.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:24.740
They are not forming citizens
for Republican self-government.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:27.230
They have largely
become madrassas

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:30.560
of higher indoctrination
that also function as the R&amp;D

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.510
department for the left.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:37.760
America needs a transformational
higher education reform agenda

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:42.080
aimed at dealing a devastating
blow to the universities

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.610
and the colleges.

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:47.810
First, the GOP should do to them
what Trump did to the media--

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.380
expose the charlatans
and humiliate them.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:54.740
No more honoring of these
corrupt institutions.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.350
Second, you go after the money.

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:01.400
America cannot continue to
subsidize these universities

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:04.760
to the tune of billions
of dollars a year.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:05.750
That means--

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:09.075
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:13.573
In other words, give your
money to Hillsdale, not

00:20:13.573 --> 00:20:14.490
to these universities.

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:15.172
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:15.172 --> 00:20:17.130
By the way, that's the
thing for conservatives.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:20.850
I'm always bewildered to see
red-blooded, patriotic, God

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:25.170
fearing conservatives giving
money to their alma mater.

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:27.690
Unless that alma mater is
one of a handful of schools

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.540
in this country that
take education seriously,

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:34.790
this is not the school
you went to 50 years ago.

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:37.780
We need to restrict
the $40 billion a year

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:39.370
we send to them
in research funds,

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.940
and we need to completely revamp
the government-backed student

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.070
loan industry.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:46.750
I'll end by saying this.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.000
I'll like to raise
the one question

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.370
I have so far not addressed,
and that, regrettably, I

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:53.560
won't have the time
to address because I'm

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.740
afraid of Anton, who wants
me to stick to my time.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:00.490
And that is, what's the positive
thing that the Republican

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.520
Party should fight for?

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:06.180
And I think that the shortest
and broadest possible answer

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.850
has to be America.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:12.270
The Republican Party should
be the party of Americans

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:16.470
who love America and
Americans, period.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:21.210
The party of unabashed
patriotic pride

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.180
in the accomplishments
of this great country.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:26.940
The party of Americans
who cling not just

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.050
to their guns and
their religion,

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:33.280
but to their capitalism, their
constitution, their land,

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:37.650
their borders, their past,
their language, their spirit.

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:42.510
The party of all those who see
themselves as Americans first.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:43.325
Thank you.

00:21:43.325 --> 00:21:46.790
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:03.400
ALLEN GUELZO: And
after the earthquake

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.260
a still, small voice.

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.606
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:18.470
When I was asked to speak to
this panel's topic, The Future

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:21.050
of the Republican Party,
the question which

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:27.540
came at once to my mind was,
why are you asking a historian?

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.430
Because my principal
preoccupation

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:32.350
is with understanding the past.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:35.730
But in the spirit of
an earlier Republican,

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.310
who suggested that
if we could first

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:42.180
know where we are and
whither we are tending,

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:46.240
we could better judge what
to do and how to do it,

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:48.430
let me take the
historical turn anyway

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.280
and ask what the
Republican Party has

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:55.270
been from its origins
in the mid-1850s

00:22:55.270 --> 00:23:00.910
before speculating on
what it will or should be.

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:04.660
What has made the
Republican Party?

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:08.560
We tend to identify the two
dominant political parties

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:11.968
by the policies
they espouse, either

00:23:11.968 --> 00:23:13.510
through their
presidential candidates

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.700
or their national platforms.

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:18.970
And almost all that we have
heard thus far this morning

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:22.810
has been about
policies, which are all

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:26.260
about the present
and the immediate.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:30.460
But that assumes that
all that makes a party

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:32.980
are its policy formulations.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:35.500
If that were the
case, American history

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:38.470
should be populated by an
exuberance of third party

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.220
movements.

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:42.030
And it isn't.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:45.630
This is because beneath
the easily visible shape

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:50.010
of policies there
exists, for both parties,

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:53.520
an iceberg-like
submergence of what may

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.560
be called political culture.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:03.750
Political culture
provides each party

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:08.470
with its long-term ballast
of values and principles.

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:13.140
Policies form the tip of the
iceberg, the visible part.

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:17.100
Political culture is what
lies below the visible,

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:21.730
but gives it its
coherence and consistency.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:23.770
This incidentally
is why third parties

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:26.590
tend to be so few
and so short-lived,

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:28.150
because third
parties are usually

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:32.980
the creatures of personalities,
or purely of policies

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:35.590
and have no ballast
of their own.

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:42.010
Political culture is the
longue durée of politics.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:45.700
For every significant
policy issue,

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.880
Republicans and Democrats
have significantly different

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:49.570
answers.

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:53.530
But the difference lies in the
different political cultures

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:56.370
from which they have emerged.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:25:00.180
So let us talk first about
the political cultures

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.760
of the parties,
and only then ask

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:06.420
what the future of the
Republican Party will be.

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:11.540
In its beginning will
be found its future.

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:14.720
The political culture
of the American parties

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:21.110
has been shaped around seven,
shall we say, arguments.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:24.780
Those seven arguments
are the economic system,

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:30.740
the political center, morals,
style, occupation, family,

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:33.080
and philosophy.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:37.130
For an economic system,
the democratic ideal

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:42.380
has always been stasis,
while the Republican word

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:45.890
has been mobility.

00:25:45.890 --> 00:25:49.820
For the political center,
the guiding democratic notion

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:55.270
has been the tribal
or the local--

00:25:55.270 --> 00:25:58.420
and that presents to us
today the form incidentally

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:00.820
of identity politics--

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:07.430
while the Republican alternative
has been the nation as a whole,

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:10.120
so that an American
identity is understood

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:15.220
as superior to a racial
or gendered identity.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:20.900
For Democrats, morals
are matters of invention,

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:25.910
while for Republicans,
morals have been natural.

00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:30.710
For Democrats, style
has been about passion,

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:35.740
while for Republicans,
style has been about reason.

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:38.830
The preferred
democratic occupation

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:43.630
has been subsistence,
and for Republicans, it

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:47.080
has been entrepreneurial.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:52.150
The democratic concept of
family has been companionate,

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:57.430
while for Republicans,
family has been organic.

00:26:57.430 --> 00:27:00.820
And in terms of
philosophy, Democrats

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:04.330
are the disciples,
even today, of Hegel

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:09.250
and the tribe of Hegel;
they are romantics,

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:12.820
while Republicans are the
disciples, even today,

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:15.370
of the moderate
enlightenment, which

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:20.420
is to say of Locke,
Madison, and Lincoln.

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:24.650
Understanding this yields
us the long-term insight

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:27.200
that we need to ask the
question of the hour

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.780
about the Republican
Party's future.

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:33.560
For in fact, the political
culture of the Republican Party

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:38.270
remains one of only two options
in American political life,

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:43.410
and will persist despite
the ephemera of policies.

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:46.400
But it will have to manage
those policies intelligently

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:50.300
and consistently with its
underlying political culture

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:53.820
if that future is to
be a successful one.

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:56.490
What will it take then
for the Republican Party

00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:02.410
to build a successful future
out of its political culture?

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:05.570
For most of
republicanism's life,

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:09.290
as either the Republican Party
or as its forerunner, the Whig

00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:13.310
Party, Democrats were
in effective control

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:15.410
of American policies.

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:17.720
That was true
during the long run

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:23.030
of the Jeffersonian/Jacksonian
Democrats from 1800 to 1860,

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:26.540
and during the even longer
run of progressive Democrats

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:31.670
from 1932 to 2016, with
the brief interregnum

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:33.680
of Ronald Reagan.

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:37.440
Under that shadow, we can
recognize fairly easily

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.100
the democratic
political culture that

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:43.130
has dictated democratic policy.

00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:48.650
Begin with the first of my seven
arguments, the economic system.

00:28:48.650 --> 00:28:52.190
Those of us here have
lived much of our lives

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.130
under the economic
direction of democrats.

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:57.950
And they, like their
Jacksonian forebears,

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:01.970
have preferred an
economy of stasis

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:07.010
in which an administrative
and corporate elite recruits

00:29:07.010 --> 00:29:12.470
a proletarian class as its ally
in corralling and suppressing

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:17.120
the middle class, who are
the paragons of mobility

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:21.320
and are seen as dangerous,
precisely for aspiring

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.120
to mobility.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.150
The evidence of this
is all around us.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:30.560
Between 1940 and 2016,
economic mobility rates

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:36.980
in the United States have
declined from 92% to just 50%.

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:43.690
We are now as immobile a
society as the United Kingdom.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:47.890
And while Democrats complain
about economic inequality,

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:52.300
they are the party who
benefits the most from it

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:57.010
and promotes the policies
most likely to ensure it.

00:29:57.010 --> 00:30:00.940
Let us take New York
City as one example.

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:04.360
Although New York City has long
been as unanimously democratic

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:10.140
as any American center can
be, 1/5 of Gotham's residents

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:13.000
live below the poverty line.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.360
The number of homeless
people in New York City

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:23.010
has increased by 37.4%
between 2007 and 2016.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:28.140
Overall, no meaningful reduction
in poverty in New York City

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:32.450
has occurred in
the last 20 years.

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:34.430
That this seems to
cause modern Democrats

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:39.180
nothing beyond rhetorical
heartburn is plain.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:44.030
Their concern about
inequality is at base a plea

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:50.060
for the reinstatement of stasis
since their forebears sneered

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:55.130
at economic mobility as a threat
to their economic privilege.

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:57.950
Republicans of an
earlier time knew

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:02.030
what this stasis was
called, and to it they

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:06.980
opposed the principles of
free labor and self-making.

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:12.370
So most Republicans of the
present day and the future.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:16.030
The promotion of that mobility
also governed Republican policy

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.830
on trade.

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:21.400
Republicans inherited from
their Whig forerunners

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:25.340
a commitment to the protection
of American commerce,

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:28.520
whether agricultural
or manufacturing.

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:32.180
Although tariffs have
often been a bad word

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:34.520
in American political
life, that is

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:39.650
because tariffs were a bad word
first for Jacksonian democrats,

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:42.960
and then for Wilsonian
progressives.

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:47.130
Partly this was because free
trade progressives had already

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:51.030
adopted the notion of a
global economy in which

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:52.890
the fate of the
American producer

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:57.090
was held to be subordinate
to the overall global system,

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:00.300
and partly because
tariffs offered benefits

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:06.630
only to the upwardly mobile,
not to an elite who had already

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:08.560
arrived.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:12.310
The founders in the first
Congress under the Constitution

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:19.470
had no hesitation about imposing
average tariffs of 81.5%.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:21.840
It was under
Democratic leaderships

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:25.720
that tariffs were
lowered to just 20%,

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:29.160
while the first Republican
tariffs under Abraham Lincoln

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:35.070
once more rose to 49% and
stayed that way until the Wilson

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:36.690
administration.

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:40.440
The purpose of the tariff
for Republicans was simple--

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:44.310
encourage the development
of the domestic economy

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:47.970
by shielding it from the
economic warfare practiced

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:50.870
by the European powers.

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:52.930
But tariffs are
only one example,

00:32:52.930 --> 00:32:55.220
and not even the most
pressing example,

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:57.350
of a larger
understanding Republicans

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:02.240
must have about the relationship
of government and the economy.

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:05.270
From the days of
Thomas Jefferson,

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:08.690
the democratic ethos has
always seen government

00:33:08.690 --> 00:33:14.150
as the restrainer, critic,
and opponent of commerce,

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:17.690
and that fundamental
attitude has not changed.

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:21.410
It did not change on the
first panel this morning.

00:33:21.410 --> 00:33:24.620
Republicans have understood
the role of government

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:28.640
as that of enabler, from
the days of Henry Clay

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:32.150
and his American system
of internal improvements

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:36.320
to Lincoln's sponsorship
of the Homestead Act,

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:39.290
the largest single
privatization of government

00:33:39.290 --> 00:33:44.090
assets in American history,
and the Pacific Railway Act.

00:33:44.090 --> 00:33:47.370
Latter day Republicans
will be in good company

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:50.040
if they understand
that commerce is

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:53.250
as much a matter of
national security as drones

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:55.520
and missiles.

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:57.610
The Republican
future will also rest

00:33:57.610 --> 00:34:02.260
on a reawakening to the
importance of morals and family

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:04.240
to its identity.

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:08.530
The prevailing nihilism,
which reduces all politics

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:11.770
to considerations
of power and places

00:34:11.770 --> 00:34:15.370
government regulatory authority
at the center of society

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:18.820
rather than families and
mediating institutions,

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:22.090
bodes well neither for
the Republican Party

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:25.330
nor for the republic itself.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:30.010
The Republicans of the 1860s
made their rallying cry

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:34.719
against slavery an
appeal to natural rights

00:34:34.719 --> 00:34:39.280
and the centrality of the
family and marriage bond.

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:43.210
We have put legalized
slavery long behind us,

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:47.500
but the arguments that the
slave holding oligarchs used

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:52.840
to defend slavery
have eerie valences

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:57.730
to those today used to
promote abortion on demand,

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:01.240
indifference to the
flourishing of families,

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:06.370
and the centralization of social
authority in an amoral elite.

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.740
Republicans of
today and tomorrow

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:13.870
must find their way back to a
national ethic of natural law

00:35:13.870 --> 00:35:16.990
morality, and with
it, a healthy division

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:22.840
of power between the state and
civil society, which is to say,

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:27.050
rediscovering their
political culture.

00:35:27.050 --> 00:35:29.500
It has been said that
Republicans cannot hope to make

00:35:29.500 --> 00:35:33.790
a case for these principles in
the face of a revolutionary new

00:35:33.790 --> 00:35:36.210
American demographic.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:39.750
Yet Republicans were facing
an even more revolutionary

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:44.310
demographic in the 1860s
when totally unrestrained

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:47.940
immigration enabled new
demographic groups--

00:35:47.940 --> 00:35:51.810
Irish, Italians, Germans--
to approach the thresholds

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:53.830
of political power.

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:57.850
Yet Republicans triumphed
because while immigrant groups

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:01.450
begin with very
distinct identities,

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:05.080
the promotion of intermarriage
and economic mobility

00:36:05.080 --> 00:36:08.890
gradually erased
those distinctives.

00:36:08.890 --> 00:36:11.290
Today we are witness
to a substantial growth

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:13.840
in Asian and Latino
voters, whom it

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:17.590
is feared Republicans have
lost to the blandishments

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:19.950
of their Democratic opponents.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.430
But Latino voters
over two generations

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:27.600
gradually lose their sense
of Hispanic particularity.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:29.340
By the third and
fourth generation

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:32.700
of Latino immigrants,
as high as 50% no longer

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:38.320
identify themselves as Latino,
but simply as American.

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:41.080
Thus, the Hispanic
and Asian electorates,

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:45.010
precisely because they are in
a state of generational flux,

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:48.650
by no means spell the end
of a Republican future

00:36:48.650 --> 00:36:52.690
any more than did their European
predecessors a century or more

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:54.190
before.

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:58.330
Already among African-Americans,
a generational shift

00:36:58.330 --> 00:37:02.650
is apparent as younger
African-Americans in 2016

00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:07.990
pulled noticeably shy of
democratic progressives

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:10.840
and continue to do so
in their chilliness

00:37:10.840 --> 00:37:14.540
toward Bernie Sanders
and Elizabeth Warren.

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:18.320
But the predictability
of this in turn

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:22.670
depends for Republicans on a
reappropriation and promotion

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:26.890
of its own political culture.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:29.800
I could perhaps go on
further into reexamining

00:37:29.800 --> 00:37:32.800
the philosophy, the
style, the occupation

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:35.390
of Republican political culture.

00:37:35.390 --> 00:37:37.940
But as Jebediah
Nightlinger immortally

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:41.660
admitted, while I have the
inclination, the maturity,

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:44.300
and the wherewithal,
unfortunately, I

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:45.620
don't have the time.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:48.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:51.850
It is worth saying, however,
that the most curiously

00:37:51.850 --> 00:37:54.400
encouraging note for
the Republican future

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:59.430
has been the vanishing of
democratic complacency.

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:01.650
It was a trademark of
the Democratic Party

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:05.340
ever since the New Deal that
the future was understood

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:08.970
to be inexorably bending
in its direction,

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:10.890
and that the task
of its intellectuals

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:14.595
was to silence
complaints about elitism

00:38:14.595 --> 00:38:19.110
and the effacement of
democracy by identity politics.

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:22.800
The election of 2016 and
the corresponding lurch

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:25.800
of the Democratic Party
beyond progressivism

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:29.160
toward European style
social democracy

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:31.410
should be seen for what it is--

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:35.520
an ominous admission
of terror at the revolt

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.520
of the deplorables.

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:42.510
The Republican Party is
the only political entity

00:38:42.510 --> 00:38:48.820
which can speak to this
revolt. If it does not,

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:54.170
the restless will turn
instead to authoritarianism,

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:59.180
and what we will get is what
Ivan Ilyin, the inspiration

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:03.530
of Vladimir Putin, calls
sovereign democracy, in which

00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:06.950
the state is unified
and centralized,

00:39:06.950 --> 00:39:10.430
its authority ceded
to an individual,

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:13.730
and that individual
glamorized and glorified by

00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:18.650
centralization, personification,
and idealization.

00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:22.090
To resist that
temptation, Republicans

00:39:22.090 --> 00:39:26.130
must find their
future in their past.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:33.900
The battle that began against
romantic oligarchy in 1861

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:36.240
is not over.

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:41.580
The truth is, we're
only in the third round.

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:44.975
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:53.240 --> 00:39:56.450
HENRY OLSEN: Thank
you very much.

00:39:56.450 --> 00:39:59.810
I want to let you know that
The Washington Post explicitly

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:03.050
hired me to provide some
balance for their page, which

00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:07.890
means on any given day I'm
outnumbered only 9 to 1,

00:40:07.890 --> 00:40:10.970
which is right
where I want them.

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:16.730
Being at a Hillsdale
Institute, Hillsdale event

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:20.210
reminds me that Hillsdale's
intellectual legacy

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.010
is descended in modern
times to its president Larry

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:26.180
Arnn from that of his
alma mater, Claremont.

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:27.920
And it would be
ill becoming of me

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:30.740
to attend anything inspired
by Claremont without quoting

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:33.500
either Aristotle or Lincoln.

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:35.600
In this case, there are
three appropriate quotes

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:38.270
that I'd like to start
with because they frame

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:41.600
very nicely what I think
the political challenge

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:43.610
of the Republican Party is.

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:45.530
First, from the
Nicomachean Ethics,

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:48.840
where Aristotle talks about
all activity having an end,

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:52.460
and he says that in
strategy, the end is victory.

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:54.530
So that for any
political party that

00:40:54.530 --> 00:40:57.680
engages in strategy, no
less than any army, the aim

00:40:57.680 --> 00:40:59.960
must be to win in politics.

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:04.010
In democratic politics,
that means to win elections.

00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:06.510
So then the question is,
how does one win elections

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:08.960
in a modern democracy?

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:12.440
Well, Abraham Lincoln
provides this quote for that.

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:17.000
In 1858, he said that public
sentiment is everything.

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:20.630
With it, nothing is forbidden,
and without it, nothing

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:22.280
can be obtained.

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:25.340
And he went ahead and
proved that to himself

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:27.470
in a private letter,
which was now

00:41:27.470 --> 00:41:30.590
part of the collection
of Lincoln writings,

00:41:30.590 --> 00:41:35.160
in which he wrote to himself
not words, but numbers.

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.760
Recall that, in
1858, senators were

00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:40.070
elected not directly by
the vote of the people,

00:41:40.070 --> 00:41:41.600
but through the legislature.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:44.540
So what he needed
to do was to win

00:41:44.540 --> 00:41:48.830
a majority of state legislators
who were to be elected.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:51.020
And this letter
contains two columns--

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:54.020
one, district by
district the votes

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:55.820
for the presidential
race, which was

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:59.300
three ways between a Republican,
a Democrat, and Millard

00:41:59.300 --> 00:42:02.630
Fillmore, the Know
Nothing or American Party

00:42:02.630 --> 00:42:06.920
candidate, and the other,
the race for governor, which

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:09.320
was a two-way, or
another race, which

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:12.830
was a two-way race between
Democrats and Republicans.

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:14.630
And through this
what he determined

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:19.040
was that the way to victory was
to persuade Know Nothings, who

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:23.390
obviously were not persuaded
by pure Republican rhetoric,

00:42:23.390 --> 00:42:26.810
to join with the Republican
cause, for if he could do that,

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:29.540
he could theoretically
win the majority.

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:33.170
So the question for us
then is to assess strength.

00:42:33.170 --> 00:42:36.290
The question is, what is
public sentiment today?

00:42:36.290 --> 00:42:38.420
Do we constitute a majority?

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:41.030
And if not, how
do we obtain one?

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:43.490
Anything else from
a political matter

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:46.250
simply pales into
insignificance.

00:42:46.250 --> 00:42:48.320
So the first thing
to recognize is

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:51.020
movement conservatives,
to use the broad term,

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:52.340
are not a majority.

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:53.840
In fact, they're
not even a majority

00:42:53.840 --> 00:42:55.760
of the Republican Party.

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:57.530
I wrote a book on
this called The Four

00:42:57.530 --> 00:42:59.510
Faces of the
Republican Party, which

00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:03.470
looked at 20 years of exit
poll data prior to Trump.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:05.900
And you can conclusively
see that that union

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:09.830
that Ted Cruz so dearly wished
for between Libertarians

00:43:09.830 --> 00:43:13.310
and Tea Partiers and
religious right establishment

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:16.250
was at most a third of
the Republican Party's

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:18.110
national electorate.

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:20.720
In fact, Cruz successfully
obtained his goal

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:22.550
and had exactly
the end I predicted

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:24.860
for him in a series of
National Review essays

00:43:24.860 --> 00:43:27.020
going back to 2014--

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:28.370
defeat.

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:32.240
He would have lost to
any sentient Republican

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:34.400
because the majority
of the Republican Party

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:38.960
exists between the
moderates who remain voters

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:42.050
in presidential primaries, even
as they are not strong enough

00:43:42.050 --> 00:43:45.110
in many of their homes, like
the San Francisco Bay area, New

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:48.860
York City, and Boston to send
representatives to Congress,

00:43:48.860 --> 00:43:52.190
and the group that tells
pollsters they are somewhat

00:43:52.190 --> 00:43:54.860
conservative, which
in a short word

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:57.800
says the people who preferred
John Boehner and Mitch

00:43:57.800 --> 00:44:01.040
McConnell to Ted
Cruz or Mike Lee.

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.120
We also know that
they're not a majority,

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:07.010
even when they contain control
of a Republican electorate

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:10.100
over broader Republican
constituencies.

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:12.110
Take the examples
of Sam Brownback

00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:14.900
and the current battles
going on in Alaska.

00:44:14.900 --> 00:44:17.030
Sam Brownback was
governor of Nebraska,

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:19.370
about his deep red a
state as you can get.

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:21.260
He pushed through
a supply side tax

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:24.260
cut praised by the Americans
for Tax Reform and virtually

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:27.260
every right thinking
intellectual on economic policy

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:28.790
in the Republican Party.

00:44:28.790 --> 00:44:31.880
And four years later,
he was defeated

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:36.020
within Republican primaries
by Republican moderates

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:37.850
and somewhat
conservatives who did not

00:44:37.850 --> 00:44:41.090
like the cuts that
this loss of revenue

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:43.220
required to public services.

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:45.920
And they joined with
Democrats to override

00:44:45.920 --> 00:44:50.000
the governor's veto repealing
the entirety of his tax cuts.

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:51.970
We're going through the
same battle in Alaska

00:44:51.970 --> 00:44:54.850
right now, where the
Governor Mike Dunleavy has

00:44:54.850 --> 00:44:57.160
said to Alaska
voters, you've been

00:44:57.160 --> 00:45:00.310
spending beyond your means
for years, you must cut back,

00:45:00.310 --> 00:45:02.980
and by doing that, I will
restore your annual check

00:45:02.980 --> 00:45:08.363
from the dividend fund of Alaska
and give you each $1,400 more.

00:45:08.363 --> 00:45:09.280
Now, think about that.

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:11.830
What he's saying is that
if you restrain spending,

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:15.820
every family of four
will get $5,600 a year.

00:45:15.820 --> 00:45:19.690
That is larger than any tax
cut that any Republican has

00:45:19.690 --> 00:45:22.510
proposed in the last 60 years.

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:27.420
And yet he sits underwater
in personal opinion ratings,

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:30.370
he's under 40%, and
there's a massive recall

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:33.550
that is forming right now
to remove him from office.

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:36.430
Even in red Alaska,
people will not

00:45:36.430 --> 00:45:40.270
choose the Republican
movement conservative economic

00:45:40.270 --> 00:45:41.950
philosophy.

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:46.270
So where is public sentiment
if it is not on the right?

00:45:46.270 --> 00:45:48.490
Well, we can look
at public sentiment

00:45:48.490 --> 00:45:52.180
by looking at its voter groups
that swing between the parties,

00:45:52.180 --> 00:45:55.510
because it is clear that it
is not democratic either.

00:45:55.510 --> 00:45:58.810
The Republican Party is
behind the Democratic Party

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:01.660
in identification, but
the Democratic Party,

00:46:01.660 --> 00:46:04.330
unlike the days of
the '50s and the '60s

00:46:04.330 --> 00:46:07.690
and the '70s when they could
command over half of America

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:11.860
as a default party loyalist,
sits barely in the 30s

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:15.010
when asked the hard question,
and only into the 40s

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:16.990
when asked it in a soft way.

00:46:16.990 --> 00:46:19.960
The Democratic Party thinks
it represents the majority,

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:23.020
but it in fact, like the
Republican Party of old,

00:46:23.020 --> 00:46:26.590
only represents the
largest mobilized minority.

00:46:26.590 --> 00:46:30.340
We can divide the swing
voters into three groups

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:32.590
if we think of it
as a menagerie.

00:46:32.590 --> 00:46:34.330
The Republicans
are the elephants,

00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:37.420
which should say, combining
all species of movement

00:46:37.420 --> 00:46:40.510
conservative with their
more moderate Boehnerite

00:46:40.510 --> 00:46:42.010
and McConnell-like brethren.

00:46:42.010 --> 00:46:43.760
These are the elephants.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:46.168
There are three swing
groups in American politics.

00:46:46.168 --> 00:46:47.710
The two that are
most prominent today

00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:50.140
I called rhinos and tigers.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:52.900
Rhinos are Republicans
in name only.

00:46:52.900 --> 00:46:57.640
They are suburban, educated,
upper income Republicans.

00:46:57.640 --> 00:47:01.550
These are the people who tended
to defect to Hillary Clinton,

00:47:01.550 --> 00:47:05.710
but remained resolutely
unsocialist in their economics.

00:47:05.710 --> 00:47:08.020
And even if they inclined
towards some degree

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:10.510
of social liberalism,
are not wholly

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:12.700
in the board of the
transformationalist

00:47:12.700 --> 00:47:16.870
progressives who wish to
undo centuries of morality.

00:47:16.870 --> 00:47:19.690
The tigers are the group
that Donald Trump attracts.

00:47:19.690 --> 00:47:22.240
I call them TIGRs because
it stands for Trump Is Great

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:22.960
Republicans.

00:47:25.495 --> 00:47:26.870
Gotta be creative
in my business.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:34.150
These are the people who
tend to be blue collar.

00:47:34.150 --> 00:47:36.000
They tend to be native
born, although there

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:39.630
are more minorities here
than you would believe

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:42.060
if you listened to the media.

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:46.410
They tend to be groups that
move towards Donald Trump

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:50.160
precisely because he was the
only Republican in decades

00:47:50.160 --> 00:47:53.423
to respond to what Bill
Galston and others spoke

00:47:53.423 --> 00:47:54.840
about on the first
panel, which is

00:47:54.840 --> 00:47:57.480
the realities of
economic life today.

00:47:57.480 --> 00:47:59.700
They like protection.

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:02.350
They like protection
from foreign labor,

00:48:02.350 --> 00:48:04.830
whether it's through
immigration restriction or trade

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:05.790
restriction.

00:48:05.790 --> 00:48:08.580
They like protection from
economic vicissitudes,

00:48:08.580 --> 00:48:11.980
which is why Donald Trump's
pledge that, unlike Paul Ryan,

00:48:11.980 --> 00:48:14.460
he was not going to touch
Social Security and Medicare

00:48:14.460 --> 00:48:18.270
was absolutely crucial to
winning these voters over.

00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:19.800
And they came in droves.

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:21.810
There were over
seven million people

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:24.810
who voted for President Obama
who voted for Donald Trump,

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:27.030
and a million to
three million more

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:29.040
who came from the
rolls of the not

00:48:29.040 --> 00:48:33.753
voting into the Republican
rolls to elect Donald Trump.

00:48:33.753 --> 00:48:35.670
They are also the reason
he swept the primary,

00:48:35.670 --> 00:48:41.040
that the Republican turnout in
the 2016 primaries and caucuses

00:48:41.040 --> 00:48:44.610
nearly equaled democratic for
the first time in history.

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.650
It was not until
the last weekend

00:48:46.650 --> 00:48:49.950
of voting when California voted
that the democratic turnout

00:48:49.950 --> 00:48:52.600
finally exceeded the
Republican turnout.

00:48:52.600 --> 00:48:56.370
People voted in the
Republican primary for Trump

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:59.550
who had never voted
for any Republican

00:48:59.550 --> 00:49:02.510
in any presidential
primary before.

00:49:02.510 --> 00:49:05.900
How do you bring the rhinos
and the tigers together?

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:08.120
Well, the rhinos are
the sort of people

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:12.260
who are supportive of
capitalist economics,

00:49:12.260 --> 00:49:15.290
but capitalism with what
you might call a human face.

00:49:15.290 --> 00:49:18.690
They are supportive
of economic cuts.

00:49:18.690 --> 00:49:20.930
They would favor
economy in government,

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:24.380
but not the wholesale
unraveling of it.

00:49:24.380 --> 00:49:26.700
They are in favor of
the traditional family.

00:49:26.700 --> 00:49:30.950
But they are not in favor
of the return to the 1950s.

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:32.690
They are not in
favor of a return

00:49:32.690 --> 00:49:37.580
to a de facto Christian America
where views on sex and gender

00:49:37.580 --> 00:49:40.880
roles are dictated by
traditional views taken

00:49:40.880 --> 00:49:43.490
from the traditional
view of the Bible.

00:49:43.490 --> 00:49:46.070
They are moderates
in that sense,

00:49:46.070 --> 00:49:49.850
neither wholly with progressives
nor wholly with conservatives

00:49:49.850 --> 00:49:51.560
on the culture war.

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:53.780
Tigers, on the
other hand, are what

00:49:53.780 --> 00:49:55.730
are called morally moderate.

00:49:55.730 --> 00:49:58.370
They will revolt on
culture wars only when

00:49:58.370 --> 00:50:00.860
they see themselves
directly skewered

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:03.090
by one side or the other.

00:50:03.090 --> 00:50:05.240
These are people who
like Donald Trump

00:50:05.240 --> 00:50:08.870
but did not like Mike
Huckabee in 2008.

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:13.760
These are people who do not
like being called deplorables

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:16.640
and do not like having
their way of life

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:20.540
attacked daily in the media
or by democratic leaders.

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:23.930
This is why sane
Democrats are hoping

00:50:23.930 --> 00:50:26.660
that Joe Biden can be the
nominee because he's not

00:50:26.660 --> 00:50:30.510
threatening to these people
on a cultural dynamic.

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:32.720
But these are people
who want protection.

00:50:32.720 --> 00:50:35.060
These are the sort of people
who wanted worker safety

00:50:35.060 --> 00:50:37.790
laws and workman
compensation back

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:40.160
in the 1900s and the 1910s.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:42.620
These are the people who were
the party of the New Deal

00:50:42.620 --> 00:50:45.560
and interpreted the New Deal
not in a socialist manner

00:50:45.560 --> 00:50:48.930
but in a humane reforming
of capitalism manner.

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:52.200
These are the people who
benefited from FHA loans.

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:54.470
These are the people who
benefited from expansions

00:50:54.470 --> 00:50:55.580
of public universities.

00:50:55.580 --> 00:50:58.100
These are the people for
whom Medicare and Social

00:50:58.100 --> 00:51:02.660
Security are the massive
anchors on which they

00:51:02.660 --> 00:51:06.440
rest their future,
non-working lives.

00:51:06.440 --> 00:51:10.130
They do not expect to depend
on family, a family which

00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:13.370
would be unlikely to support
them if these things were gone.

00:51:13.370 --> 00:51:16.370
They do not depend on
local institutions.

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:19.370
They depend on Social
Security and Medicare

00:51:19.370 --> 00:51:23.560
for a comfortable,
dignified retirement.

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:25.780
A Republican Party
that can speak to these

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:28.960
is a Republican Party
that can gain them.

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:31.630
Right now, we have two
Republican parties,

00:51:31.630 --> 00:51:35.350
both of which are essentially
warring over minority

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:36.200
coalitions.

00:51:36.200 --> 00:51:39.760
The Trump coalition, which is
the elephants plus the tigers,

00:51:39.760 --> 00:51:42.640
is a coalition that is
electorally efficient

00:51:42.640 --> 00:51:45.340
because tigers tend to
live in the Upper Midwest,

00:51:45.340 --> 00:51:47.470
and they can swing
the electoral college.

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:49.940
And because of the way
our Senate is elected,

00:51:49.940 --> 00:51:51.940
you can have pretty much
a lock on the Senate,

00:51:51.940 --> 00:51:54.760
as long as you have an
elephant and tiger majority.

00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:58.450
You cannot win the House with
this majority because it has

00:51:58.450 --> 00:52:02.140
nothing to the rhinos that swung
the house over to the Democrats

00:52:02.140 --> 00:52:03.310
last time.

00:52:03.310 --> 00:52:05.590
The traditional Republican
Party effectively

00:52:05.590 --> 00:52:09.470
wants a rhino plus
elephant coalition.

00:52:09.470 --> 00:52:11.170
This is the Romney coalition.

00:52:11.170 --> 00:52:13.150
This was the Paul
Ryan coalition.

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:15.550
This was a coalition that
can deliver the house

00:52:15.550 --> 00:52:18.700
and deliver it rather well,
even without gerrymandering.

00:52:18.700 --> 00:52:21.940
It may not be a large majority,
but it is a decent chance

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:24.190
to have a long-term majority.

00:52:24.190 --> 00:52:26.620
It is one for which
the Senate is a coin

00:52:26.620 --> 00:52:30.220
flip because it is more
suburban and it is less rural.

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:33.130
And it is one that has no hope
of winning the presidency.

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:37.270
They claim otherwise, but I only
have two minutes and 30 seconds

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:41.450
and I don't want to spend it
demolishing their argument.

00:52:41.450 --> 00:52:43.030
The short-term
Republican majority

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.910
is to combine the three
together-- rhinos, tigers,

00:52:45.910 --> 00:52:47.860
and elephants, oh my.

00:52:47.860 --> 00:52:53.200
This is something that a
modified Trumpism, one shorn

00:52:53.200 --> 00:52:58.570
of the president's
distinctive negative aspects

00:52:58.570 --> 00:53:01.900
of his personality, but one
that is more open to what I call

00:53:01.900 --> 00:53:05.680
a limited and energetic
government, one that

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:08.650
is willing to act,
not simply stand back

00:53:08.650 --> 00:53:12.030
in the face of the realities
of the modern economic state,

00:53:12.030 --> 00:53:16.990
and one that values security
as in equal measure to liberty

00:53:16.990 --> 00:53:20.620
and opportunity, that is
where the majority is.

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:23.860
And that is something that a
left wing influence did not

00:53:23.860 --> 00:53:27.400
dominate the Democratic Party
would have very difficult time

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:29.380
competing against.

00:53:29.380 --> 00:53:31.930
The last group is one that
I've never mentioned before--

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:33.910
Michael gets a
new animal today--

00:53:33.910 --> 00:53:35.670
and that is the ram.

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:36.970
What is a RAM?

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:40.160
The Recently Arrived Minority.

00:53:40.160 --> 00:53:44.980
See, I had to have a nice
animal for immigrants.

00:53:44.980 --> 00:53:47.680
Who are the rams?

00:53:47.680 --> 00:53:54.190
The rams are unlike
other immigrant groups.

00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:58.690
And in our history, they are
distinctly Asian and from Latin

00:53:58.690 --> 00:53:59.440
and South America.

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:01.750
A non-trivial
number from Africa.

00:54:01.750 --> 00:54:06.280
What they are is perhaps
the least Christian

00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:08.470
of any of our immigrant groups.

00:54:08.470 --> 00:54:12.010
Hispanics are dramatically
lapsed Catholics.

00:54:12.010 --> 00:54:14.770
And virtually no
Asian immigrant comes

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:16.810
from a Christian background.

00:54:16.810 --> 00:54:19.450
They are also from more
collectivist backgrounds.

00:54:19.450 --> 00:54:22.570
And this they do share in
common with the second wave

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:25.810
of immigration, the people
from Italians and Eastern

00:54:25.810 --> 00:54:30.910
Europe who shaped the 1880
to 1820 migration wave.

00:54:30.910 --> 00:54:34.600
They will over time become
assimilated to American ways

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:38.890
of life and consequently be
more open to American arguments.

00:54:38.890 --> 00:54:42.310
But the fact remains that
even today the grandchildren

00:54:42.310 --> 00:54:45.460
and the great grandchildren
of the immigrants of that wave

00:54:45.460 --> 00:54:49.030
are much more open to
economic intervention

00:54:49.030 --> 00:54:52.000
than the wave of people
who are descended

00:54:52.000 --> 00:54:55.010
from native British
Protestant stock.

00:54:55.010 --> 00:54:59.180
A Republican Party that wants to
engage with demographic change

00:54:59.180 --> 00:55:03.110
has to engage with the
rams on their own turf.

00:55:03.110 --> 00:55:05.930
In that sense, they are merely
a different manifestation

00:55:05.930 --> 00:55:09.440
of the challenge of engaging
with rhinos and tigers.

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:13.280
And it is perhaps
an act of hutzpah

00:55:13.280 --> 00:55:16.370
to invoke the name of Lincoln
with Allen Guelzo sitting

00:55:16.370 --> 00:55:17.730
to the right of me.

00:55:17.730 --> 00:55:19.280
But I think that
the Republicanism

00:55:19.280 --> 00:55:24.070
of Lincoln and Reagan, one
of a limited but energetic

00:55:24.070 --> 00:55:27.670
government, one that sees
action and inaction as two

00:55:27.670 --> 00:55:31.270
sides of the same coin
with the thumb firmly

00:55:31.270 --> 00:55:34.300
on the scales of liberty,
but not so firmly

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:38.020
on the scales of liberty to
leave one blind to reality,

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:39.910
that one can engage with this.

00:55:39.910 --> 00:55:41.980
And if one does
this, then I think

00:55:41.980 --> 00:55:45.010
there is a more than passing
chance that the Republican

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:49.540
Party can do something it has
not been able to do since 1928,

00:55:49.540 --> 00:55:52.630
and that is to
genuinely say that they

00:55:52.630 --> 00:55:56.620
command the plurality, if
not the majority, support

00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:59.394
of the voters the United
States of America.

00:55:59.394 --> 00:56:05.855
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:24.490 --> 00:56:26.520
MICHAEL ANTON: Here we go.

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:30.110
So being the chair, I get to
ask the first questions, one

00:56:30.110 --> 00:56:33.010
of the privileges
of being the chair.

00:56:33.010 --> 00:56:36.280
Now, I'm going to try to
split the difference in a way

00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:38.172
from the presentations
I have heard,

00:56:38.172 --> 00:56:39.880
take a little bit from
each, and then see

00:56:39.880 --> 00:56:41.300
how they respond to this.

00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:43.870
So starting with Dave.

00:56:43.870 --> 00:56:46.900
When I look at the
recent electoral numbers,

00:56:46.900 --> 00:56:49.270
I think Azerrad is
of course correct

00:56:49.270 --> 00:56:52.480
that recent immigrants and
their immediate descendants

00:56:52.480 --> 00:56:56.500
from the post-1965
wave tended to vote

00:56:56.500 --> 00:56:58.870
in rough terms
two-to-one democratic.

00:56:58.870 --> 00:57:01.510
And so if the demographic
change continues

00:57:01.510 --> 00:57:03.640
the way it's
changing, that doesn't

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:07.510
spell great electoral things
for the Republican Party.

00:57:07.510 --> 00:57:11.665
And to quote Marco Rubio
from one of the 2016 debates,

00:57:11.665 --> 00:57:13.450
"they know exactly
what they're doing."

00:57:13.450 --> 00:57:15.310
That is, the Democrats
who are pushing this,

00:57:15.310 --> 00:57:17.950
opposing the wall, opposing
any kind of reform,

00:57:17.950 --> 00:57:19.780
and in fact getting
more radical as we

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:21.580
watch the democratic debates
in this season-- getting

00:57:21.580 --> 00:57:24.122
more radical in their insistence
that the country just simply

00:57:24.122 --> 00:57:26.842
has to be wide open
to anyone forever.

00:57:26.842 --> 00:57:28.300
On the other hand,
Professor Guelzo

00:57:28.300 --> 00:57:30.092
is certainly correct
that the United States

00:57:30.092 --> 00:57:33.730
has undergone significant
demographic change very rapidly

00:57:33.730 --> 00:57:38.410
in high numbers in the
past, and has successfully

00:57:38.410 --> 00:57:41.270
assimilated these populations
and turned many of them

00:57:41.270 --> 00:57:41.770
Republican.

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:46.120
In fact, built Republican
majorities in part

00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:49.540
on the strength of voting
patterns of recent immigrants,

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:52.630
or at least their descendants
one or two generations down.

00:57:52.630 --> 00:57:55.600
So during the 2016
campaign, one of the things

00:57:55.600 --> 00:57:57.940
that I'll be just
blunt and confess,

00:57:57.940 --> 00:57:59.990
I was a fairly early
Trump supporter.

00:57:59.990 --> 00:58:01.628
I wasn't one of
those who at the end

00:58:01.628 --> 00:58:03.170
had to hold my nose
and vote for him.

00:58:03.170 --> 00:58:05.218
I did it sort of
enthusiastically.

00:58:05.218 --> 00:58:06.760
And one of the
reasons for that was I

00:58:06.760 --> 00:58:11.022
thought, well, he's breaking
the old Republican mode where

00:58:11.022 --> 00:58:13.480
the working class, the lower
middle class, the blue collar,

00:58:13.480 --> 00:58:17.410
the non-college educated,
sees the Republican

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:20.970
Party as simply just out to get
them, not for their interests.

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:23.950
The party of finance,
the party of outsourcing,

00:58:23.950 --> 00:58:26.920
the party of cutting
benefits, as Henry mentioned.

00:58:26.920 --> 00:58:29.560
And that party had a
very limited appeal,

00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:31.960
to say the least,
to this demographic.

00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:34.270
So it seemed like Trump
was the first to come along

00:58:34.270 --> 00:58:37.970
who was really going to
break that mold, maybe

00:58:37.970 --> 00:58:41.830
with the prospect of a
long-term realignment

00:58:41.830 --> 00:58:45.400
of that economic segment
of the electorate aligning

00:58:45.400 --> 00:58:46.540
with the Republican Party.

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:48.910
But given the reality
of demographic change,

00:58:48.910 --> 00:58:52.660
as it's ongoing in this
country, the Republican Party

00:58:52.660 --> 00:58:54.370
could maybe
successfully do that,

00:58:54.370 --> 00:58:57.040
but it won't successfully win
elections or build a majority

00:58:57.040 --> 00:58:59.680
unless it can get a
pretty significant chunk.

00:58:59.680 --> 00:59:02.320
It doesn't have to be 50%,
but a pretty significant chunk

00:59:02.320 --> 00:59:07.160
of Black, Hispanic, blue collar
workers, voters, and so on.

00:59:07.160 --> 00:59:10.900
And so if the goal is to get
to at least a plurality that's

00:59:10.900 --> 00:59:13.790
bigger than the other guy's
plurality, or even better yet,

00:59:13.790 --> 00:59:17.020
an enduring majority
in the low to mid-50's,

00:59:17.020 --> 00:59:18.790
the Republican Party
has to do that.

00:59:18.790 --> 00:59:21.010
Or I just don't see how it
survives as a party that

00:59:21.010 --> 00:59:22.320
can win national elections.

00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:24.730
So my quick question then
for all three panelists

00:59:24.730 --> 00:59:27.530
is, what are the
prospects for success.

00:59:27.530 --> 00:59:32.060
And if you're the chair of
the RNC, what do you do?

00:59:32.060 --> 00:59:34.060
What are the things that
you would put in place,

00:59:34.060 --> 00:59:37.770
have the party do, in order to
make that success more likely?

00:59:37.770 --> 00:59:40.720
We'll just go in order, I think.

00:59:40.720 --> 00:59:42.860
DAVID AZERRAD: I don't
have a proper answer

00:59:42.860 --> 00:59:47.990
to that serious and
important question, Mike.

00:59:47.990 --> 00:59:50.240
And I'm not speaking for the
Heritage Foundation here.

00:59:50.240 --> 00:59:53.510
I'm speaking as a Canadian
immigrant to the US.

00:59:53.510 --> 00:59:56.060
What gives me pause
about immigration is not

00:59:56.060 --> 00:59:58.160
the immigrants--
it's the elites.

00:59:58.160 --> 01:00:01.040
I mean, this is just
not our country anymore.

01:00:01.040 --> 01:00:02.510
Immigrants will assimilate.

01:00:02.510 --> 01:00:04.280
You have no choice
but to assimilate.

01:00:04.280 --> 01:00:07.454
The issue is
assimilate into what?

01:00:07.454 --> 01:00:09.530
Into Howard Zinn's
America, going

01:00:09.530 --> 01:00:11.090
into our schools,
our university,

01:00:11.090 --> 01:00:12.620
our general cultural,
teaching them

01:00:12.620 --> 01:00:15.470
that this is the country
that invented slavery

01:00:15.470 --> 01:00:17.240
and Jim Crow that
is irredeemably

01:00:17.240 --> 01:00:19.730
racist and sexist
and homophobic,

01:00:19.730 --> 01:00:21.830
encouraging them to
think of themselves

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:24.380
as hyphenated Americans because
that's where the goodies are

01:00:24.380 --> 01:00:26.810
from the government, the racial
preferences, the handouts,

01:00:26.810 --> 01:00:28.530
the you name it?

01:00:28.530 --> 01:00:32.450
There is an anecdote I like to
share that Indians from India

01:00:32.450 --> 01:00:34.550
petitioned the Reagan
administration to be

01:00:34.550 --> 01:00:36.290
reclassified as Asians.

01:00:36.290 --> 01:00:38.720
They were classified as Whites.

01:00:38.720 --> 01:00:39.930
They demanded to be Asians.

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:40.430
Why?

01:00:40.430 --> 01:00:44.120
Because there are government
benefits if you are a minority.

01:00:44.120 --> 01:00:46.650
You get special loans from
minority-owned businesses.

01:00:46.650 --> 01:00:53.090
So I am very much in favor
of slowing down immigration

01:00:53.090 --> 01:00:58.580
because I do not trust in us, in
our capacity, in our willpower,

01:00:58.580 --> 01:01:02.510
to assimilate immigrants
into Americanness.

01:01:02.510 --> 01:01:06.110
And independently of any
electoral considerations,

01:01:06.110 --> 01:01:07.460
that gives me a lot of pause.

01:01:07.460 --> 01:01:10.550
Until we regain confidence,
until we reconquer

01:01:10.550 --> 01:01:13.630
the institutions we
lost to the left,

01:01:13.630 --> 01:01:17.420
we're asking to simply
swell the ranks of people

01:01:17.420 --> 01:01:21.140
who are not going to think in
the right American way because

01:01:21.140 --> 01:01:21.770
of us.

01:01:21.770 --> 01:01:24.537
I think they may come here
with the best of intentions.

01:01:24.537 --> 01:01:26.620
We're going to encourage
them to go the other way.

01:01:30.742 --> 01:01:34.620
ALLEN GUELZO: Couple of
thoughts come to mind here.

01:01:34.620 --> 01:01:37.080
Invoking Lincoln
on public sentiment

01:01:37.080 --> 01:01:41.070
is an interesting
way of understanding

01:01:41.070 --> 01:01:43.170
Lincoln as a political figure.

01:01:43.170 --> 01:01:45.150
Public sentiment, he
said, is everything.

01:01:45.150 --> 01:01:47.340
With it, you can do
a great many things.

01:01:47.340 --> 01:01:50.070
Without it, nothing.

01:01:50.070 --> 01:01:52.890
But he did not regard
himself as being passive

01:01:52.890 --> 01:01:54.810
in the face of public sentiment.

01:01:54.810 --> 01:01:57.910
When he talked about
public sentiment that way,

01:01:57.910 --> 01:02:01.370
his idea was you shape
public sentiment.

01:02:01.370 --> 01:02:04.172
And you shape it around
a certain set of values.

01:02:04.172 --> 01:02:05.630
One does not see
him in the debates

01:02:05.630 --> 01:02:09.860
against Stephen Douglas
attempting to temperize,

01:02:09.860 --> 01:02:13.500
to modify, to tone down.

01:02:13.500 --> 01:02:16.970
If anything, over the course
of the seven debates of 1858,

01:02:16.970 --> 01:02:19.260
Lincoln becomes more trenchant.

01:02:19.260 --> 01:02:21.150
He becomes sharper.

01:02:21.150 --> 01:02:26.460
So that by the last two of
the great debates of 1858,

01:02:26.460 --> 01:02:29.490
Lincoln is now speaking
in broad, great terms

01:02:29.490 --> 01:02:31.290
about the principles
that are at stake,

01:02:31.290 --> 01:02:33.990
in slavery versus freedom.

01:02:33.990 --> 01:02:37.500
And he does not hesitate
to compare slavery

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:39.810
to the rule of kings.

01:02:39.810 --> 01:02:41.230
He says, it really
doesn't matter

01:02:41.230 --> 01:02:43.120
whether you're talking
about a king who

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:45.610
seeks to bestride the
people of his own nation

01:02:45.610 --> 01:02:47.560
and live by the
fruit of their labor,

01:02:47.560 --> 01:02:50.830
or one race of men as an apology
for enslaving another race.

01:02:50.830 --> 01:02:53.200
It is the same
tyrannical principle.

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:55.360
He believed in shaping
public opinion.

01:02:55.360 --> 01:02:57.512
And he believed the
long-term shaping was

01:02:57.512 --> 01:02:58.720
going to make the difference.

01:02:58.720 --> 01:02:59.220
[FIRE ALARM]

01:02:59.220 --> 01:03:01.770
SPEAKER: May I have
your attention, please.

